<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alidius Tjarda van Starkenborgh Stachouwer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1888">1888</a>–<a href="/years/1978">1978</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alidius Tjarda van Starkenborgh Stachouwer was governor-general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a> from 1936, the last before the Japanese conquest. A conservative diplomat, he presided over a colony that rejected nationalist demands for autonomy such as the Soetardjo petition of 1936. He stayed at his post when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> invaded in 1942 and spent the rest of the war in Japanese internment. He later served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and representative to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alidius-tjarda-van-starkenborgh-stachouwer.jpg" alt="Alidius Tjarda van Starkenborgh Stachouwer" width="320" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Willem van de Poll · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alidius_Warmoldus_Lambertus_Tjarda_van_Starkenborgh_Stachouwer_(1935).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tjarda, as sources often call him, came from an old noble family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groningen">Groningen</a>. His father, Edzard Tjarda van Starkenborgh Stachouwer, was mayor of Groningen, a senator and Queen's commissioner of the province, and his mother, Christine Jacobe Quintus, was a music teacher and opera soprano. He studied law at the University of Groningen from 1906 to 1911 and entered the diplomatic service. While posted to Washington he married Christine Marburg, daughter of Theodore Marburg, in Baltimore in November 1915. He later served as Queen's Commissioner for Groningen from 1925 to 1933, succeeding his father, and as envoy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>A royal decree of 4 June 1936 appointed him the 69th governor-general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>, and he took office in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a> on 16 September 1936. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> on 14 May 1940, he declared martial law, had 19 German cargo ships seized and interned German nationals. Japanese bombers were attacking Batavia by 15 February 1942, and the government moved to Bandoeng. On 5 March he separated civilian from military power and handed command to General Hein ter Poorten, then met Lieutenant General Hitoshi Imamura of the 16th Army on 8 March and ordered the Allied troops to cease fire the next day.</p><p>Because he never surrendered civilian authority at the Capitulation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalijati">Kalijati</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> could transfer it to Lieutenant Governor-General Hubertus van Mook in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, the basis of the Netherlands Indies Civil Administration in exile. Refusing special treatment, he was held in Bandoeng and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a> and finally in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>, alongside the American general Jonathan M. Wainwright, until the Soviets liberated the camp on 16 August 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After a difference of opinion with Johann Heinrich Adolf Logemann, the minister for the overseas territories, he resigned with effect from 16 October 1945, and the office of governor-general ceased to exist. He later led the first permanent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> representation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> from March 1952 to mid-1956, taking part in preparations for a European Political Community, and became Minister of State on 28 June 1956. He headed the talks with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> on canal links between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, concluded by a treaty on 13 May 1963. He died on 16 August 1978 and was buried in Wassenaar.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alidius Tjarda van Starkenborgh Stachouwer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groningen">Groningen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. Died in Wassenaar, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.22,6.57&amp;year=1946" aria-label="Groningen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Alidius Tjarda van Starkenborgh Stachouwer"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5336 5562h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5336 5562h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.22,6.57&amp;year=1946">What was here in 1946? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
